## Orchestrator Monitoring Patterns

`task-board spawn` is background execution with tracking. Do not wrap it in shell backgrounding (`&`, `nohup`, runtime `run_in_background`) just to avoid blocking; the command already returns once the tracked runner is registered.

### Pattern A: Fan Out Tracked Background Runs

```bash
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
task-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
task-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role tester --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
```

Each call returns a `RUN-...` identifier plus a task- and run-scoped logwork path. Concurrent runs for the same task and role therefore keep separate log files and outcome resources. The runner enforces shared concurrency from `task-board.config.json -> spawn.max_parallel` (default `20`), so you can queue more runs than the machine is allowed to execute at once.

### Pattern B: Replay And Follow Event Cursors

```bash
task-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
task-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
task-board spawn observe RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5 --until terminal --format compact
task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn directives RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn directive RUN-260320-abc123 cancel
task-board spawn goal RUN-260320-abc123
```

Every valid explicit spawn prints these monitor commands automatically after queueing, seeded with cursor `RUN-ID:0` and bound to the same `task-board` executable / board context that launched the run.
In JSON mode the same contract is returned under `observation`:
`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, and `inSessionDelivery`.
`observation.runtime` reports the orchestrator session runtime (`codex-session`, `claude-session`, or `external`) and `observation.delivery` names its non-blocking completion channel (`codex-in-session`, `claude-background-wait`, or `external-watch`).

Use the printed `observe --until terminal --format compact` command as the
default single surface: it consumes events newer than its durable cursor and
appends terminal checklist/outcome handoff evidence without replaying earlier
history. Use `status` for a diagnostic snapshot, `events` for replay, `watch`
for follow mode, and `wait` only for a barrier that does not need event/handoff
detail. Do not stack these reads on the routine success path.
In JSON mode, `spawn watch` uses notification NDJSON framing only (one notification object per line, no trailing summary payload).

### Pattern C: Durable Directive Plane

- `spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` records a durable operator directive with acknowledgement state.
- `spawn directives RUN-...` replays the directive history for that run.
- `cancel` is applied immediately for queued runs and terminates the background runner/process group for running runs when the platform supports it.
- `pause_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, and `nudge` are cooperative signals. Current runtimes expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`; the child may poll `task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"` at safe checkpoints.
- `restart` and `reroute_after_checkpoint` create successor `RUN-...` records instead of rewriting existing run history. Running reroute/pause requests are rejected when the runtime cannot checkpoint safely in place.
- Both directive commands print the current active goal header before directive details. New records persist the immutable goal ID/revision under which they were issued; legacy records print `issued_under_goal: unknown`.
- Restart and a reroute that preserves the current goal ID, revision, role binding, scope, kind, predicate, and intent remain owner-agent recovery actions. Goal-bound `cancel` and non-monotonic reroute are operator-authoritative controls and require a non-empty reason. A structured replacement validates its requested scope/kind/predicate against the successor role before it writes the directive or successor.

### Pattern D: Goal-Bound Delivery Pool

After plan approval and before the first producer launch or delivery status transition, start one independently tracked orchestrator owner:

```bash
task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth \
  --role orchestrator \
  --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b \
  --background \
  --agent codex \
  --model gpt-5.6-sol \
  --reasoning-effort max
```

The CLI resolves containers to an active dependency-closed task/bug pool,
derives `delivery_pool/accepted_done` from the normalized orchestrator role,
reserves the RUN/GOAL lineage, validates provider capability and the
primary-session launch plan, and only then permits normal launch side effects.
The Session Manager attaches the exact `run/RUN-*` owner, reconciles its native
goal, and submits the assignment only after the session's applied revision
matches the authoritative v2 head. Nested specialists receive role-derived
`role_handoff` or `reviewer_verdict` owners without replacing the parent
`delivery_pool` condition.

The authoritative state is the `board-goal-v2` `run/RUN-*` owner head and its
immutable revision history. `manifest.launch_goal`, `state.active_goal_ref`,
and run-local revision files are one-release `board-goal-v1` compatibility
mirrors written with the v2 promotion. Failed native activation leaves the
prior head active; an unbound head is `pending-unbound` until the provider
acknowledges that exact revision.

Parent and run owner heads are isolated manager slots. The spawned provider
environment strips parent manager/app-server endpoints, auth indirections and
their token values, and parent provider session markers. Persisted caller-plane
fields are decode-only compatibility data; new launches issue no caller RPC.
One-release restoration remains only for an active legacy manifest that
already contains an exact caller snapshot.

At every checkpoint:

```bash
task-board spawn goal "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
```

If a nested target resolves outside the current pool, `spawn` returns non-zero `goal_scope_mismatch` before assignment, prompt, manifest, provider, or child-process effects. The payload classifies missing leaves as `adjacent`, `outside`, or `mixed` and includes the exact optimistic update command. Run that command only when the work is genuinely required, then retry the spawn.

Nested spawn also fails closed on its declared parent execution context. While
a legacy already-running Claude owner has `successor_required`, the command
returns typed `goal_provider_binding_pending` with `side_effects=false`; new
goal-bound Claude owners start directly in manager-held PTYs. If the declared
parent manifest, state, or active revision cannot be loaded, the command
returns `goal_parent_context_unavailable` rather than treating the caller as
non-goal.

Owner-agent changes are monotonic: create with `--scope`, expand with `--add-scope`, refresh only the non-authoritative intent, and supply `--if-revision` for updates. Upsert responses expose `mutation.previous_revision` as `0` on creation, the prior active revision on a new immutable revision, and the current revision on an idempotent replay. Restart and same-semantics reroute retain that latest revision under a rebound successor owner. Scope removal, structural replacement, weakening, kind changes, and cancellation cross the operator directive boundary and create durable cancelled/superseded lineage rather than successful delivery.

For a legacy already-running Claude run, `successor_required` retains its
one-release safe-checkpoint behavior. New manager-owned sessions do not use
that split path.

Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch or transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet producer handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer branches, and unmet delivery acceptance persist a structured recovery cause and automatically queue a successor with the same active goal revision. The predecessor is superseded by recovery, never successful delivery.

If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, its manifest and state
remain non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start cause, retry time,
and scheduler lease. Status, wait, and follow observers claim an eligible or
expired lease under the scheduler lock, retry that same successor outside the
lock, and clear the deferred marker only after runner start/heartbeat. The
predecessor may already be superseded, but the same immutable goal revision
still has exactly one retryable owner instead of two terminal runs.

Provider `blocked` and a role that exits at board `blocked` use the same
Stop-The-Line gate. Board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped evidence
packet terminates the run with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; reviewer runs
also persist the `stop_the_line` verdict. This branch never satisfies delivery
acceptance. Without the status/evidence pair, the boundary claim is recoverable
and the same revision is queued again.

Producer provider exit is not handoff success. A `role_handoff` run must reach its immutable role end status and add or update a task-scoped outcome artifact relative to its launch snapshot. Zero exit while the board remains in `analysis`/`development`, or at the handoff status without evidence, queues autonomous same-revision recovery.

After attaching the task-scoped outcome, producers should use
`task-board handoff TASK-ID --role ROLE`. The command resolves the configured
role end status, verifies every checklist item and an actual stored task-scoped
outcome, then performs the evidence decision and ordinary status transition
under one mutation lock. It reports all missing checklist/outcome evidence and
leaves status unchanged when the gate fails. Spawn-log artifacts do not count as
producer outcomes, and the runtime still enforces its launch-relative outcome
snapshot independently.

Delivery success is not provider completion alone. Reviewer goals persist exactly one evidence-backed branch derived from board state: `accepted`, `changes_requested`, or `stop_the_line`; an exit while still `reviewing` triggers recovery. `required`/`light` leaves need board `done`, task-scoped producer outcome evidence, and a later reviewer whose persisted branch is `accepted`. `none` leaves need `done`, producer outcome evidence, and the owner’s completion-time evidence check.

### Pattern E: Delivery Boundary (Watcher vs In-Session)

- `spawn events`, `spawn watch`, and `spawn observe` are external watcher surfaces built on persisted run events.
- They can notify a human/operator and feed TUI/remote push adapters.
- They do **not** inject inbound text into the currently active orchestrator session by default.
- When the orchestrator session is launched through `task-board codex`, tracked terminal state is also injected back into the active Codex thread through the app-server runtime adapter.
- `task-board codex` may also auto-steer safe `waitingOnUserInput` pauses inside that active thread when `session_manager.providers.codex.auto_continue.enabled=true` or `--auto-continue` is set for the launch.
- The v1 anti-stall controller never auto-continues `waitingOnApproval` and does not try to answer child-process terminal prompts on behalf of the operator.
- If runtime-native direct push is unavailable, use terminal delta observation (`spawn observe --cursor ... --until terminal --format compact`) as the default; replay (`spawn events --cursor ...`) and snapshot checks (`spawn status`) remain diagnostic alternatives.

These are observation/session enhancements only. They do not determine whether
either primary launcher can create a goal-bound tracked owner.

### Pattern F: Claude Orchestrator Non-Blocking Fan-Out

Claude Code has no injection API for a live session, so there is no Claude
counterpart of the Codex app-server push adapter. The equivalent non-blocking
channel is the harness itself: Claude Code re-invokes the session when a
tracked background command exits. Spawn detects a Claude session through the
`CLAUDECODE` environment variable and reports
`observation.delivery = claude-background-wait`.

The orchestrator pattern:

1. Spawn all independent children first — each fully selected tracked spawn returns its `RUN-...` immediately.
2. For every returned run, start the printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` command as a **harness background task** (Claude Code `Bash` with `run_in_background`), not as a blocking foreground call.
3. Keep orchestrating: the session stays free while children execute; each background wait exits when its run reaches terminal state, and the harness wakes the session with that result.
4. On each wake-up, inspect `spawn status RUN-...` / outcome resources and route the next step (reviewer spawn, rework, dependent task) — then return to waiting on the remaining runs.

```bash
# One Claude orchestrator, five concurrent children, zero blocking:
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5   # -> RUN-A
task-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5   # -> RUN-B
# ... spawn remaining children ...
# then one harness background task per run:
#   run_in_background: task-board spawn wait RUN-A
#   run_in_background: task-board spawn wait RUN-B
```

Do not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` itself in a background helper — `--background`
already returns a tracked run. Only the `spawn wait` observation barrier goes
into the harness background slot. Sequenced work (producer -> reviewer) still
uses an explicit `spawn wait` barrier before spawning the dependent child.

Single-child variant: the fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` collapses
steps 1–2 into ONE harness background Bash call per child — it registers the
tracked run identically and then blocks in-process to terminal state, so its
exit wakes the session exactly like a standalone background `spawn wait` (see
"Fused Spawn And Wait" above). Wrapping that single call in `run_in_background`
remains the caller/harness decision — task-board cannot make it.

### Removed: Launch-Time Foreground Mode

The old launch-time foreground/attached harness (the original foreground `task-board spawn TASK --wait`, hidden `--attached`, and proposed `--foreground`) has been removed from the public spawn path. It mixed execution placement with observation and made the parent/orchestrator act like a worker.

The current `--wait` flag is not that mode returning: it is a fused observation barrier on top of the unchanged tracked background runner (see "Fused Spawn And Wait" above). Execution placement stays background-only — `--background` is required with or without `--wait`.

Use tracked background spawn first, then wait on the `RUN-...` handle when a blocking barrier is needed:

```bash
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
```

The postmortem for that removal is attached to `TASK-260619-spid7i` as `TASK-260619-spid7i_foreground-harness-postmortem.md`.

---

## Template-Based Prompt Composition

Spawn always composes the prompt from the role template:

1. Reads task README (title, description, scope, acceptance criteria)
2. Reads precondition resources from progress.md
3. Loads each resource's content from `.task-board/.resources/`
4. Renders everything through a Go template

The resolved `agent_context.profile` selects one of two embedded templates:

- `full` is the backward-compatible default. It renders the historical role
  body, CLI reference, run-control, saving, safety, completion, and workdir
  sections unchanged.
- `lite` renders a compact invariant/command role contract. It keeps the exact
  FIRST/LAST lifecycle commands, merged task+role DoD, lazy skill catalog, task
  metadata/preconditions, task-scoped outcome command, literal `to-review`
  handoff wording, reviewer verdict branches, compact Stop-The-Line evidence
  packet, and workdir. Selection, checklist injection, completion-hook evidence
  enforcement, run tracking, and reviewer routing remain board/runtime gates
  outside the presentation template.

Because a lite worker receives one concrete element and its task inputs, its
prompt uses compact task-specific projections and skips routine `summary()`,
`plan()`, `schema()`, and `{ full }`. Scoped schema remains available after an
unknown call; broad routing and audit reads remain Orchestrator/full-profile
tools.

The **full default template** is embedded in the binary and includes:
- Task metadata (ID, title, description, scope, AC, parent)
- Precondition resource contents as "Instructions" sections
- Board CLI reference (status updates, checklist, notes, resource saving)
- Explicit instructions to save results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` for revisions
- Task-scoped artifact naming guidance such as `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`
- Logbook guidance for important findings, decisions, anomalies, or regressions
- Completion-hook contract: if a spawned task lands in `to-review` without a new task-scoped outcome artifact, the hook leaves a blocking note and reverts the task to its start status

**Project-local template overrides** are profile-specific:

- `.agents/spawn-template.md` for `full`
- `.agents/spawn-template-lite.md` for `lite`

Both expose the same data:
```
{{.TaskID}}                    — element ID
{{.TaskTitle}}                 — from README title
{{.TaskDescription}}           — from README description
{{.TaskScope}}                 — from README scope
{{.TaskAC}}                    — from README acceptance criteria
{{.ParentID}}                  — parent element ID
{{.AgentName}}                 — agent display name (e.g. "[implementer] developer (claude)")
{{.BoardDir}}                  — path to .task-board/
{{.PreconditionResources}}     — slice of {Name, Description, Content}
{{.RoleName}}                  — role name (e.g. "developer") — empty if no --role
{{.RoleDescription}}           — compact description from role.md frontmatter
{{.RoleBody}}                  — markdown body from role.md
{{.Archetype}}                 — "implementer", "analyst", "reviewer", "tester"
{{.Skills}}                    — slice of {Name, Path} — resolved lazy skill catalog refs
{{.StartStatus}}               — status to set on launch (e.g. "development")
{{.EndStatus}}                 — status to set on completion (e.g. "to-review")
{{.DoDContent}}                — merged DoD: task-specific board checklist + role baseline DoD
```

**Workflow for spawning:**
```bash
# 1. Put task-specific DoD on the board
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text="OAuth2 flow works end-to-end")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text="Token refresh handles edge cases")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text="Rate limiting returns 429")'

# 2. Link precondition resources (optional — provides additional context)
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Implementation guide")'

# 3. Spawn with explicit role/model/reasoning selection
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max

# 4. Agent runs, saves results
# (agent runs: task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Implementation notes")')
```

---

## Prerequisites

### Claude (`claude -p`)
1. **Claude Code installed:** `which claude` → available in PATH
2. No git or trust requirements — works in any directory

### Codex (`codex exec`)
1. **Codex CLI installed:** `which codex` → `/opt/homebrew/bin/codex` (or similar)
2. **Git repo:** `codex exec` requires a git repository. Will fail with `Not inside a trusted directory and --skip-git-repo-check was not specified` if not in a git repo
3. **Trust configured:** Project must be trusted in `~/.codex/config.toml` or use `--skip-git-repo-check`
4. **Model access:** Check which models work with your account type (see Failure Modes below)

---

## Manual Launch (Fallback)

Use manual launch only when `task-board spawn` is not available or you need custom control.

### Step 1: Write prompts to files

**CRITICAL:** Never pass prompts inline via heredoc + `&` background. The shell loses context. Always write prompts to files first.

```bash
mkdir -p .temp/prompts
# Write each agent's prompt to a separate file
# .temp/prompts/codex-agent-name.md
```

### Step 2: Launch via stdin redirect

**Claude:**
```bash
claude -p \
  --output-format json \
  --model opus \
  --dangerously-skip-permissions \
  < .temp/prompts/claude-agent-name.md
```

**Key claude flags:**
- `-p` — non-interactive "print" mode (reads prompt from stdin, no REPL)
- `--output-format json` — structured output
- `--model MODEL` — model to use (opus, sonnet, etc.)
- `--max-budget-usd N` — budget cap in USD
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` — no approval prompts

**Codex:**
```bash
codex exec \
  -m gpt-5.6-sol \
  -c 'model_reasoning_effort="max"' \
  --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox \
  --skip-git-repo-check \
  -C /path/to/project \
  - < .temp/prompts/codex-agent-name.md \
  > .temp/codex-agent-name.log 2>&1 &
echo "PID: $!"
```

**Key codex flags:**
- `-m MODEL` — explicit model (inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'`)
- `-c 'model_reasoning_effort="EFFORT"'` — explicit model-compatible effort; `xhigh` is Extra high, `max` is the highest recommended board-managed level, and `ultra` remains an explicit compatible delegation choice on supported models
- `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` — run non-interactively without Codex sandbox so spawned agents can spawn further tracked children
- `--skip-git-repo-check` — bypass trust check when the project is intentionally outside a normal git checkout
- `-C DIR` — working directory (must be the project root)
- `- < file.md` — read prompt from stdin (the `-` is required!)
- `> .temp/log 2>&1 &` — background with log capture

Codex CLI 0.142.0 no longer accepts the old `-a never` shorthand on
`codex exec`; use `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` for automation.

### Step 3: Verify within 30 seconds

```bash
# Check logs are growing
sleep 30 && wc -c .temp/codex-*.log

# Check task-board updates
task-board q --format compact 'agents()'
```

If logs are 0 bytes or not growing → agent is dead. See Failure Modes.

### Step 4: Monitor

```bash
# Log sizes (are they growing?)
wc -c .temp/codex-*.log

# Process check (are they alive?)
ps aux | grep "codex exec" | grep -v grep | wc -l

# Board updates
task-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'

# Last modification times
for f in .temp/codex-*.log; do
  stat -f "%Sm %N" -t "%H:%M:%S" "$f"
done
```

---

## Failure Modes (Field-Tested)

### 1. Empty output — heredoc + background

**Symptom:** Log file is 0 bytes, codex exits immediately with exit code 0.

**Cause:** Passing prompt inline with `$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)` and running with `&` background. The shell subprocess loses the heredoc context.

**Fix:** Write prompts to files, use `- < file.md` stdin redirect.

### 2. "Not inside a trusted directory"

**Symptom:** Log says `Not inside a trusted directory and --skip-git-repo-check was not specified`

**Cause:** Project directory is not a git repo or not in codex trust config.

**Fix:** `git init && git add -A && git commit -m "init"` before launching codex.

### 3. "Model not supported with ChatGPT account"

**Symptom:** Log says `{"detail":"The 'codex-5.3' model is not supported when using Codex with a ChatGPT account."}`

**Cause:** Some older or gated models require API access, not ChatGPT Plus/Pro subscription.

**Fix:** Inspect the installed live registry with `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'`, then retry with an explicitly available model and compatible effort. Do not depend on `~/.codex/config.toml` defaults.

**How to check:** Run a quick test: `codex exec -m MODEL --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox --skip-git-repo-check "echo hello" 2>&1 | head -20`

### 4. Context exhaustion — agent reads too much, does too little

**Symptom:** Agent logs show it reading large files (.research/ methodology docs, 600+ lines), doing web searches, then process exits. No output files written. Task stays in `analysis` status forever.

**Cause:** ChatGPT account codex has limited context/turns. Agent spends all context on reading methodology + web searching, never reaches the "write results" step.

**This is the most insidious failure mode** because the agent appears to work (logs grow, task-board updates happen) but produces no deliverables.

**Fix options:**
1. **Compact prompts** — Don't tell agents to read large methodology files. Put essential context directly in the prompt
2. **Smaller scope** — One task per agent, not three. Better to spawn 9 agents doing 1 task each than 3 agents doing 3 tasks
3. **Skip methodology** — Tell agent "Do NOT read .research/ files — go straight to work"
4. **Use tracked Claude spawn for research** — reroute through `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --role researcher --background` so the child still has a tracked `RUN-...` handle, role lifecycle, and outcome-resource contract while using Claude's larger context

### 5. Agent updates task-board but doesn't finish work

**Symptom:** `task-board agents` shows agent as active with recent updates, but agent actually exited. Tasks are stuck in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` with no real blocker.

**Cause:** Agent ran the first lifecycle commands, then exited before completing the actual work or reaching its role handoff status. Board state looks active even though execution stopped.

**Fix:** Always cross-check: `task-board q 'agents()'` + `task-board spawn status RUN-...` + run events/log file modification times. If the process is dead but the task did not reach its producer handoff or reviewer verdict status, inspect run events and outcome resources, then retry or reroute with a focused child run. Use `blocked` only when the evidence proves an external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, and attach the full evidence packet plus the exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Never hand recoverable work to the human or leave a parked intermediate status as the final state.

---

## Prompt Template for Codex Agents

Optimized for codex's limited context — compact, no file reading, concrete IDs.

```markdown
## FIRST COMMAND — RUN IMMEDIATELY

task-board m 'assign(TASK-260212-XXXXXX, agent=codex-NAME); set_status(TASK-260212-XXXXXX, status=analysis)'

---

## Task Board Protocol

For EACH task: assign, set analysis, do work, attach outcome evidence, then set to-review. NEVER set done unless you are the reviewer role. If your final board status is to-review, report "ready for review" or "handed off to review"; do not say the task is done/complete/finished/final/готово. If objectively blocked, set blocked with concrete evidence instead of parking in analysis/development/reviewing.

---

## Your Job

[2-3 sentences of context. DO NOT tell agent to read large files.]

## Tasks:

1. TASK-260212-XXXXXX: task-name
   - [What to do — be specific]
   - After producing handoff evidence: task-board handoff TASK-260212-XXXXXX --role developer

2. TASK-260212-YYYYYY: next-task
   - task-board m 'assign(TASK-260212-YYYYYY, agent=codex-NAME); set_status(TASK-260212-YYYYYY, status=analysis)'
   - [What to do]
   - After producing handoff evidence: task-board handoff TASK-260212-YYYYYY --role developer
```

**Rules:**
- Keep total prompt under 100 lines
- No references to large files (>200 lines)
- Put essential context inline, not by reference
- Max 3 tasks per agent (ideally 1-2)
- Concrete IDs only — never placeholders

---

## Cleanup After Failed Agents

```bash
# Kill all codex processes
pkill -f "codex exec"

# Reset task statuses
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=backlog); unassign(TASK-260218-abc)'

# Reset story statuses
task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc, status=backlog); unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'

# Verify clean state
task-board q --format compact 'summary()'
task-board q --format compact 'agents()'  # should show no active codex agents
```

---

## Choosing Agent Type

Board task work must go through tracked spawn with explicit role/model selection;
Codex and Qwen also need an explicit compatible effort. Runtime-native helper
agents can still exist for non-board exploratory chat when the human explicitly
requests inline execution, but they are not an execution path for tracked board tasks.

| Factor | `spawn --agent claude` | `spawn --agent codex` | `spawn --agent qwen` | Native helper agents / Task tool |
|--------|----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|-------------------------------|
| Board integration | Automatic (spawn) | Automatic (spawn) | Automatic (ordinary tracked runs) | Not allowed for board task execution |
| Role support | `--role` flag (auto status, DoD, skills) | Same | Same | No board role lifecycle |
| Model validation | Explicit model; reasoning rejected | Explicit model + compatible effort | Explicit model + compatible effort | N/A |
| Goal-bound owner | Supported | Supported | Not supported | No board goal lifecycle |
| Prompt persistence | Auto-saved to `.temp/` | Auto-saved to `.temp/` | Auto-saved to `.temp/` | In conversation context only |
| Model discovery | `models(agent=claude)` | `models(agent=codex)` | `models(agent=qwen)` | Runtime-specific |
| Billing metadata | Provider API | OpenAI API / ChatGPT | Qwen Token Plan Team metadata in registry | Runtime-dependent |

**Recommendations:**
- Resolve `preferred_agentic_system` and the active schedule window first. With no canonical restriction, Claude/Codex runtime affinity remains the fallback; select Qwen explicitly or through canonical/scheduled policy.
- Use Codex when you want OpenAI model diversity or Codex-specific behavior. Query the current registry; the July 2026 lineup runs from `gpt-5.6-sol`/`terra`/`luna` through `gpt-5.5`, `gpt-5.4`, `gpt-5.4-mini`, and `gpt-5.3-codex-spark`, each with an explicit compatible effort.
- Use Qwen only with a registered Qwen model and explicit supported effort; ordinary child tracking is supported, but a `--goal-scope` owner must use Codex or Claude.
- Do not use native helper agents / Task tool for board task work. If it belongs on the board, it needs a tracked `RUN-...` handle from a fully explicit spawn.
- For research-heavy board tasks, prefer `task-board spawn TASK-... --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --role researcher --background` when Claude's context is the deliberate choice.

---

## Real-World Example: What Went Wrong

**Project:** MTS Tariff Research, Phase 1 — 6 parallel research stories.

**Attempt 1:** `codex exec -m codex-5.3` — Model not supported with ChatGPT account. All 6 agents died instantly. Empty logs.

**Attempt 2:** `codex exec -m gpt-5.2` — Model works, agents start, update task-board, read methodology files, do web searches. But all 6 run out of context before writing output files. Zero deliverables.

**Historical note:** Phase 0 used Claude Code Task tool with opus model — 3 parallel agents, each produced 500-700 line research documents in ~6 minutes. That was useful before tracked background spawn was the board contract, but it is no longer the recommended board execution path.

**Lesson:** For research-heavy board tasks (web search + read docs + synthesize + write large output), prefer tracked `spawn --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --role researcher --background` over untracked native helpers or context-limited Codex runs.

